# Portfolio Editor - AI Context & Architecture

This file provides context for any AI assistant (like Cursor, GitHub Copilot, or Gemini) working on this project.

## Project Architecture
- **Frontend:** React 18, Vite, Tailwind CSS, Lucide React (Icons), dnd-kit (Drag & Drop).
- **Backend:** Express.js (Node.js) running on port 3000.
- **Storage:** Local file system (`/data` directory) for state and images.
- **Cloud:** Cloudflare R2 for public hosting and image delivery.

## Dual-Environment Constraint (CRITICAL)
This application must run flawlessly in TWO environments:
1. **Web-based Preview (AI Studio/Cloud Run):** Runs behind an Nginx reverse proxy.
2. **Local Windows 11 Machine:** Runs via a local Node.js server (`start_windows.bat` or `run_fast.bat`).

### Rules for Cross-Environment Compatibility:
- **File Paths:** ALWAYS use relative paths with forward slashes (e.g., `./data/images/` or `../assets/`). NEVER use Windows-specific absolute paths (e.g., `C:\...`).
- **CORS & Fetching:** The frontend runs on the same origin as the backend API (`http://localhost:3000`). API calls should use relative paths (e.g., `fetch('/api/state')`).
- **File Headers:** When generating code, include the exact relative file path as a comment at the top of the block (e.g., `// File: ./src/App.tsx`).

## Data Structure (`/data` directory)
- `state.json`: The single source of truth for the portfolio. Contains all posts, titles, descriptions, and media links.
- `uploads/`: Legacy manually uploaded images.
- `flickr/` & `instagram/`: Legacy images scraped from social media.
- `originals/`: High-resolution source files placed manually by the user.
- `highres/`: Legacy processed large versions of the originals.
- `backups/`: Automated backups of `state.json`.

## Media Variant Strategy (CURRENT)
- New media processing must use these derived variants whenever a clean source image exists:
  - `thumb400`: 400px longest edge
  - `1k`: 1024px longest edge
  - `2k`: 2048px longest edge
  - `3k`: 3072px longest edge
  - `original`: source/original file
- Do **not** upscale images. If a source is too small for a target size, skip that variant and log the gap.
- Preferred new JSON media fields:
  - `image_thumb`
  - `image_1k`
  - `image_2k`
  - `image_3k`
  - `image_original`
- Legacy fields must remain readable for compatibility:
  - `image`
  - `image_large`
  - `image_3k`
  - `url`
  - `largeUrl`
  - `link`

## Video Thumbnail Contract (Bunny Stream) — ALWAYS
Every media item of `type: "bunny"` MUST have exactly this shape:

- `videoId`, `libraryId` — Bunny identity (always present).
- `url` AND `image_original` — ALWAYS the embed URL:
  `https://iframe.mediadelivery.net/embed/{libraryId}/{videoId}`.
  Never store an image here, even when a custom thumbnail is used.
- `bunnyThumbUrl` — ALWAYS set to the deterministic CDN thumbnail:
  `https://iframe.mediadelivery.net/{libraryId}/{videoId}/thumbnail.jpg`.
  This is the guaranteed fallback that does not depend on R2 sync.
- `image_thumb`, `image_1k`, `image_2k`, `image_3k`, `image` — the variant
  set with the SINGLE canonical base name `bunny-{videoId}`:
  `bunny-{videoId}_thumb.jpg`, `bunny-{videoId}_1k.jpg`, etc.
  `image_2k`/`image_3k` may be "" when the source is too small (never upscale).
  In published state these MUST be absolute R2 URLs
  (`https://pub-85bb68a84f3b4ba6b512b3d165c96497.r2.dev/v2/data/uploads/...`).
- `image_width`, `image_height` — real source resolution.
- `custom_thumb` — ONLY when the user picked a custom thumbnail (optional);
  if set, it wins in the UI, but the `image_*` set and `bunnyThumbUrl` stay intact.

FORBIDDEN:
- Base names `vidbunny-{timestamp}*` for anything stored long-term.
- `image_original`/`url` pointing to an image on a bunny item.
- Persisting `bunnyThumbUrl: ""` — always compute it.

FRONTEND RESOLUTION ORDER (single rule for feed/lightbox/reorder):
`custom_thumb` → `image_thumb` → `image` → `image_1k` → `bunnyThumbUrl`
→ `image_original` (only if it is an image, never if it contains `mediadelivery.net/embed/`).
Feed cards render bunny items as an autoplaying iframe; the thumbnail is a fallback/poster.

NORMALIZATION:
Any code that saves/publishes state must pass bunny items through the
normalizer (sets embed URLs, `bunnyThumbUrl`, best `image`). New thumbnails
are always generated under `bunny-{videoId}` and uploaded to R2.

## Versioned Storage Strategy
- Keep the existing Cloudflare R2 bucket.
- Store all newly generated media in a new versioned path prefix: `v2/`.
- Never delete or overwrite legacy media only to migrate structure.
- Frontend and backend should prefer `v2` assets, but remain compatible with legacy references.
- Example local structure for new generated assets:
  - `data_v2/uploads/thumbs400/...`
  - `data_v2/uploads/1k/...`
  - `data_v2/uploads/2k/...`
  - `data_v2/uploads/3k/...`
  - `data_v2/uploads/originals/...`
- Example R2 structure for the same assets:
  - `v2/data/uploads/thumbs400/...`
  - `v2/data/uploads/1k/...`
  - `v2/data/uploads/2k/...`
  - `v2/data/uploads/3k/...`
  - `v2/data/uploads/originals/...`

## Cloudflare R2 & GitHub Integration
The app syncs data to a Cloudflare R2 bucket to serve the public portfolio.
- **Bucket Name:** `portfoliodata`
- **Public Domain:** `https://pub-85bb68a84f3b4ba6b512b3d165c96497.r2.dev`
- **Sync Logic:** The `server.ts` handles uploading files to R2 using the `@aws-sdk/client-s3` library. The `index.html` file generated during "Publish" is also uploaded here.
- **GitHub Connection:** If Cloudflare R2 is unavailable or as an alternative backup, the system can sync the `state.json` and `index.html` to a GitHub repository. GitHub Pages can be used as an alternative hosting provider for the static portfolio.
- **AI Exchange File:** `Connect_front_back.md` is the operational hand-off channel between frontend and backend/scraping AI agents. Use it for migration notes, missing-image reports, ratio/format changes, and frontend fallback requirements.

## Key Features & Workflows
- **Scraping:** Fetches images from Flickr/Instagram and saves them locally.
- **High-Res Sync:** Scans `data/originals/` for high-res versions of scraped images. Matches are based on filenames. Uncertain matches are flagged for manual review.
- **Cloud Sync:** Mirrors all local images to Cloudflare R2.
- **Publish:** Generates a static `index.html` injecting the current `state.json` data and uploads it to R2.
- **Batch Migration:** Existing assets may be backfilled into the new variant structure. Missing or defective sources must be logged instead of silently upscaled.

## Batch Files
- `start_windows.bat`: Checks for `node_modules`, installs dependencies if missing, and starts the server.
- `run_fast.bat`: Starts the server directly without checking dependencies, and closes the batch window automatically.

## Shortcut Commands for AI
- `#plan`: Do not write code. Analyze the request, create a step-by-step architecture plan, list affected files, and assess risk (1-10).
- `#exec [step]`: Execute a specific step from the plan.
- `#review`: Analyze the chat history/code for errors or deviations from clean code principles.
- `#check`: Assess the complexity of the current task (1-10). If > 7, recommend switching to a more advanced model.

## Logging Rule For AI
- If `Connect_front_back.md` is updated with new operational notes, missing files, or compatibility warnings, explicitly tell the user in the final response.

## Restart Recommendation Rule (ALWAYS)
After every code change made by the AI, end the response with a bold restart recommendation line in this format:
`**Nächster Schritt: Bitte starte <EMPFEHLUNG>, um die Änderungen zu übernehmen.**`

Use this decision logic:

1. Recommend `start_windows.bat`, when:
   - dependencies changed (`package.json`, lockfiles, requirements, runtime/tooling setup),
   - environment/runtime setup was changed,
   - user returns after a long break and wants a safe restart.

2. Recommend `run_fast.bat`, when:
   - backend logic changed (`server.ts`, Python scripts, API logic),
   - data structure stayed compatible (no reinstall/setup needed).

3. Recommend `Nur Localhost/Browser Refresh`, when:
   - only frontend files changed (`src/*.tsx`, `src/*.ts`, styles, static UI),
   - dev server is already running and hot reload is sufficient.

Priority for mixed changes:
- If conditions from multiple groups apply, use the safest option in this order:
  `start_windows.bat` > `run_fast.bat` > `Nur Localhost/Browser Refresh`.

## Restart Execution Rule (ASK FIRST)
After giving the restart recommendation, always ask the user if the AI should execute that step now.

Use this exact follow-up question style:
`Soll ich das jetzt direkt für dich ausführen?`

Execution behavior:
- Only execute after explicit user confirmation.
- For `start_windows.bat` or `run_fast.bat`, run the selected batch file via terminal.
- For `Nur Localhost/Browser Refresh`, do not claim browser control; instead instruct the user to refresh manually.
